      "knowledge_point": "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem",
      "problem": "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $\\text{ ______ }$ on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\text{ _________ }$, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III, listed below, be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\nI. continuous\n\nII. $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively\n\nIII. defined",
      "worked_solution": "The intermediate value theorem states the following.\n\n*Let $f(x)$ be a function that is **continuous** on a closed interval $[a,b].$ If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(a)$ **and** $f(b)$ **inclusively**, then there exists a number $c\\in [a,b]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II\".",
